us":"candidate_requires_semantic_review"}],"identification":{"ambiguity_reason":"","artifact_refs":["GTC_000003_000571_000645_f331ce3b40dee73b","GTC_000003_001030_001104_3954e6b08d816dce"],"boundary":"exclusive_before:turn_000003","candidate_artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000571_000645_f331ce3b40dee73b","GTC_000003_001030_001104_3954e6b08d816dce"],"family_id":"FGF_d1b38bee049b2a5cd0149be1","family_id_provenance":{"algorithm":"sha256","canonical_input":{"artifact_ids":["GTC_000003_000571_000645_f331ce3b40dee73b","GTC_000003_001030_001104_3954e6b08d816dce"],"case_id":"CASE_21DFA2A0B7DA"},"canonicalization":"json-sort-keys-compact-utf8","case_id_included":true,"digest_hex":"d1b38bee049b2a5cd0149be17eef4a8ae3b6d48d256de72db7197646d7ee0d8b","digest_prefix_length":24},"family_label":"OpenAPI YAML examples contrasting oneOf and anyOf schema validation","rationale":"Both candidates are coherent, response-local YAML schema examples generated together in the first assistant response to explain the distinction between OpenAPI oneOf and anyOf.","response_event_index":3,"response_turn_id":"turn_000003","status":"complete","target_prompt_event_index":2,"target_prompt_id":"turn_000002"}},"first_generation_response":{"create_time":1686059665.318481,"event_index":3,"role":"assistant","source_node_id":"af133b34-fd3f-40cd-a5a0-d4371b155863","source_parent_id":"aaa2d509-aa4f-40aa-8b97-6006867b519c","source_record_index":3,"text":"In OpenAPI, `oneOf` and `anyOf` are JSON Schema keywords used to define the validation rules for request or response payloads. They are used to specify alternative schemas that a given value must conform to.\n\nHere's the difference between `oneOf` and `anyOf`:\n\n1. `oneOf`: It specifies that a value must match exactly one of the provided schemas. In other words, the value must adhere to the constraints of exactly one of the defined schemas. If a value matches multiple schemas, it would be considered invalid. It enforces a mutually exclusive choice.\n\nExample:\n```yaml\noneOf:\n  - type: string\n    maxLength: 5\n  - type: number\n    minimum: 10\n```\nIn the above example, the value must either be a string with a maximum length of 5 or a number greater than or equal to 10.\n\n2. `anyOf`: It specifies that a value must match at least one of the provided schemas. The value can conform to any of the defined schemas, allowing for multiple valid options. If a value matches multiple schemas, it is considered valid.\n\nExample:\n```yaml\nanyOf:\n  - type: string\n    maxLength: 5\n  - type: number\n    minimum: 10\n```\nIn the above example, the value can be a string with a maximum length of 5 or a number greater than or equal to 10. It can satisfy either condition.\n\nTo summarize, `oneOf` enforces a single, exclusive choice among the provided schemas, while `anyOf` allows for multiple valid options by matching any of the provided schemas.","turn_id":"turn_000003"},"target_developer_prompt":{"create_time":1686059647.980315,"event_index":2,"role":"user","source_node_id":"aaa2d509-aa4f-40aa-8b97-6006867b519c","source_parent_id":"29cc5c9e-cb04-4978-bca0-9f4a17fd3337","source_record_index":2,"text":"what's the difference between openapi oneOf vs anyOf ?","turn_id":"turn_000002"}},"csv_extraction":{"context":[{"category":"context","evidence_spans":["what's the difference between openapi oneOf vs anyOf ?"],"item_id":"context_1","normalized_text":"The question concerns the OpenAPI schema composition keywords `oneOf` and `anyOf`.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"specificity":[{"category":"specificity","evidence_spans":["what's the difference between openapi oneOf vs anyOf ?"],"item_id":"specificity_1","normalized_text":"Explain the difference between OpenAPI `oneOf` and `anyOf`.","source_roles":["user"],"source_turn_ids":["turn_000002"]}],"verification":[]},"identity":{"case_id":"CASE_21DFA2A0B7DA","conversation_id":"954082b4-d72d-4b5f-a0d1-4a2107705ddb","conversation_url":"https://chat.openai.com/share/954